Dear freshman me. You're going to spend a semester scared of the word "transformer" because everyone says it like it's a religion. Here's the thing nobody tells you: you already do the core trick every time you read.

## The trick is called attention

When you read "the bank was steep so we slid down to the river," you don't panic about whether "bank" means money. Your brain quietly checks the neighbors — *steep*, *slid*, *river* — and picks the right meaning. That check-the-neighbors move is attention. A transformer just does it with numbers:

- every word gets turned into a vector (a long list of numbers)
- each word "asks" every other word how relevant it is
- the answers get blended into a new, context-aware vector

Do that a few dozen times in a row and the model stops seeing words and starts seeing *meaning*. That's most of the magic. Really. The famous paper is literally called [Attention Is All You Need](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762).

## The part that humbled me

I assumed the hard part would be the math. It wasn't — it's like three matrix multiplications wearing a trench coat. The `softmax(QK^T / sqrt(d))V` you keep seeing? Q asks, K answers, V delivers. In NumPy it's honestly just:

```python
scores = Q @ K.T / np.sqrt(d)   # every word asks every word
weights = softmax(scores)       # who actually matters here?
context = weights @ V           # blend the answers together
```

The hard part is believing something so simple scales into something that writes poetry.

> Science takes time. Mind your business.
— me, on this very website

## What I'm doing about it

1. Reimplementing attention from scratch in NumPy (no peeking at libraries)
1. Breaking it, on purpose, to see which parts actually matter
1. Writing the mess down here so future me can laugh at present me

More findings soon. The lamp is on and the pencil is sharpened.
